aw, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{21-8}{28} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{28}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-12551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d20eeb8b56b42bb3f2cd469a7a29d4ee756cf637f8c6d9aa05b7717029d02da5"} {"question" "q-12551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-12551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{12}{23}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{28}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-12551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{13}{28}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-87308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{18}{25}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{4}{5},$ find $P(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-87308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{18}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-18}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-87308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d20eeb8b56b42bb3f2cd469a7a29d4ee756cf637f8c6d9aa05b7717029d02da5"} {"question" "q-87308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-87308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{25}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-87308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{25}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{8}{21}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $P(A).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{8}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{63-32}{84} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{84}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d20eeb8b56b42bb3f2cd469a7a29d4ee756cf637f8c6d9aa05b7717029d02da5"} {"question" "q-120524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{13}{84}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{23}{84}"] ["math" "\\frac{31}{84}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{31}{84}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A game show uses a roulette wheel divided into $50$ panels. There are $15$ blue, $15$ green, $15$ yellow, $2$ black, and $3$ white panels randomly shuffled on the roulette wheel. What is the probability that the roulette wheel stops on a white or green panel?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $W$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a white panel, and let $G$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a green panel. Then the probability that the roulette stops on a white or green panel given by $P(W\\cup G).$\n\nGiven that the roulette will stop at only one panel, the events $W$ and $G$  are mutually exclusive, i.e., $P(W\\cap G)=0.$ \n\nWe can find $P(W)$ and $P(G)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $50$ panels in the roulette. Of those panels, $3$ are white, and $15$ are green. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =\\frac{3}{50}. \\\\ P(G) & =\\frac{15}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\cup G) & =P(W)+P(G)-P(W\\cap G) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{50}+\\frac{15}{50}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d20eeb8b56b42bb3f2cd469a7a29d4ee756cf637f8c6d9aa05b7717029d02da5"} {"question" "q-5616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{18}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{50}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{25}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-12554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A card is drawn from a standard $52$-card deck. The drawn card is the Ace of Spades and it is set aside. A second card is drawn from the deck. What is the probability that the drawn card is an ace or a spade?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck has $4$ suits with $13$ cards in each suit. Spades is one of the suits.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-12554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let us call $A$ the event \"The second card drawn is an ace,\" and $S$ the event \"The second card drawn is a spade.\" The required probability is given by  $P(A\\cup S).$ \n\nGiven that the Ace of Spades is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $S$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(A\\cap S)=0.$\n\nSince the Ace of Spades was set aside, there are $51$ cards remaining,  of which $3$ are aces and $12$ are spades. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{3}{51}, \\\\ P(S) & =\\frac{12}{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup S) & =P(A)+P(S)-P(A\\cap S) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{51}+\\frac{12}{51}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+12}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-12554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d20eeb8b56b42bb3f2cd469a7a29d4ee756cf637f8c6d9aa05b7717029d02da5"} {"question" "q-12554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-12554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{14}{51}"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{52}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{51}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{52}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{17}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-12554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{17}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A number is picked from $10$ to $19.$ What is the probability of picking an even number or a prime number?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $E$ be the event that the picked number is an even number, and let $R$ be the event that the picked number is a prime number. Then the probability that the selected number is an even number or a prime number is $P(E\\cup R).$\n\nGiven that all prime numbers between $10$ and $19$ are odd, $E$ and $R$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(E\\cap R)=0.$\n\nWe can find $P(E)$ and $P(R)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $10$ numbers from  $10$ to $19$, of which  $5$ are even, and $4$ are prime.   Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E) & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\ P(R) & =\\frac{4}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E\\cup R) & =P(E)+P(R)-P(E\\cap R) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{4}{10}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d20eeb8b56b42bb3f2cd469a7a29d4ee756cf637f8c6d9aa05b7717029d02da5"} {"question" "q-120526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On a shelf, there are $11$ novels and $9$ science books. $1$ of the novels and $3$ of the science books are illustrated. The illustrated novel is taken from the shelf and set apart. If a single book is selected at random, what is the probability that it is a novel or an illustrated book?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $I$ be the event that the selected book is an illustrated book, and let $N$ be the event that it is a novel. Then the probability that the selected book is a novel or an illustrated book is given by $P(I\\cup N).$\n\nSince the only illustrated novel was removed, there are no novels left on the shelf that are also illustrated. Therefore, $I$ and $N$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(I\\cap N)=0.$\n\nSince the illustrated novel was set apart, there are $10+9=19$ books remaining, of which $10$ are novels and $3$ are illustrated. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I) & =\\frac{3}{19}, \\\\ P(N) & =\\frac{10}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I\\cup N) & =P(I)+P(N)-P(I\\cap N) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{19}+\\frac{10}{19}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+10}{19} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d20eeb8b56b42bb3f2cd469a7a29d4ee756cf637f8c6d9aa05b7717029d02da5"} {"question" "q-120545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{13}{19}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{20}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{19}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-120545" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{13}{19}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Kevin is choosing a random name for his new character in a video game. If all names may only start with an uppercase letter, what is the probability that the generated name will start with an \"R\" or an \"E\"?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $R$ be the event that the name starts with an \"R,\" and let $E$ be the event that the name starts with an \"E.\" Then the probability that the name will start with an \"R\" or an \"E\" is given by $P(R\\cup E).$\n\nGiven that only one letter can be picked as the first letter, $R$ and $E$ are mutually exclusive events. i.e. $P(R\\cap E)=0.$\n\nThere are $26$ letters in the alphabet, and each letter has an equal chance of being randomly picked ($1$ out of $26$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =\\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ P(E) & =\\frac{1}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R\\cup E) & =P(R)+P(E)-P(R\\cap E) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}+\\frac{1}{26}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{26} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d20eeb8b56b42bb3f2cd469a7a29d4ee756cf637f8c6d9aa05b7717029d02da5"} {"question" "q-5617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{13}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{26}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{13}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{13}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{26}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "q-5617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{13}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/state.json" "file_sha256" "5d293e9ea1e1e93ba2387c003d22b146f94c69ee205546af210f9fe99cd41cb9"} {"question" "e-5727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ $P(B)=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{7}{12},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/example-5727.json" "file_sha256" "c49509f6a267926a3ca23d8d4b5dbf6a534e8d8b3a9d2370d27b6faafee2a73f"} {"question" "e-5727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{7}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{7}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+3-7}{12} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/example-5727.json" "file_sha256" "c49509f6a267926a3ca23d8d4b5dbf6a534e8d8b3a9d2370d27b6faafee2a73f"} {"question" "e-5634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(A\\cup B) = \\dfrac{4}{9},$ find $P(B).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/example-5634.json" "file_sha256" "47947a1f3da01c4ab7332b47761bed57d7d5c19e8da3642be371366062b86620"} {"question" "e-5634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-3}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/example-5634.json" "file_sha256" "47947a1f3da01c4ab7332b47761bed57d7d5c19e8da3642be371366062b86620"} {"question" "e-5636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Two cards are drawn from a standard ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c1a5898916d3156aa9acb1884f2ab9fd149c10ca2149574a50fc6147586691e6.png)-card deck: the ace of hearts and the queen of hearts. These are set aside, and a third card is drawn from the remaining deck. What is the probability that the third drawn card is an ace or a heart?\n\n*Hint: A standard ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/5f378bdc2525ccdfd98ae36dc698e3bdf3f328a76d6841faf2198ec37b34db9a.png)-card deck has ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/d44ac804f533a60a0d252fbcddaec9693d8b989dc429bf03f3ef1f0587cf3453.png) suits with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/f3ae95c18ff411808c146e5d3bfc30f49380b8272511f1a90a3400209b2df340.png) cards in each suit.There is one ace in each suit.\n Hearts is one of the suits.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/example-5636.json" "file_sha256" "e30910b7778992a15cc5ad18fb9d993f342cf99a2dddf931becdfd15f4f78487"} {"question" "e-5636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/15fa693fff5bc0eb2a8851089612b5a3fb969c3fd5fbc7c0821e58e52ff4260d.png) be the event that the third card is an ace, and let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/86056525c7e84fd98e233f12873f4b7bd6ed728224c7c1afe986743f6cda0aa0.png) be the event that the third card is a heart. Then the probability that the third card is an ace or a heart can be represented as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/a143776333b936a72708b71c7deadb626e347a6c094630c9dbce894d550ed5bb.png)\n\nGiven that the ace of hearts is no longer in the deck, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/3397629141c3034fbd4b5cd5bd8c554676175a4b1e3f5eccd26cc5d66ee1bec8.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/d18d46bb8861930c4c3e602e4eef58228e745671a5445823a11d088e0a9e43a8.png) are mutually exclusive events, i.e., ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/37f1cba75404eaf490f8ae5f3de37c1da606e372b6b2e1f1c9c2c23a9ee0f82d.png)\n\nWe can find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/3abd50b1d24b25f8908834ec0b3ef94c630f1c8c5352c0528a381b4bddc30ed1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/a73f6c30e5a00859bc7f04c5f08a59a978dcc714634187dd178a9fa2059d1578.png) by computing the necessary ratios. When the third card is drawn, there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c6852fb3b1ffe57a198559e8fb2ffc12bd834126e291117f7158395501b7dbc5.png) cards in the deck. Among the remaining ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c6852fb3b1ffe57a198559e8fb2ffc12bd834126e291117f7158395501b7dbc5.png) cards, there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/97314fc826b205001aa477df5cccb4ceb42aff037d8ccf663a40341a70629d9f.png) hearts and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/6da8ea22dec2b00f0f9fa815118a83e3877bfdba5149ea487f242a1839878b0e.png) aces. Therefore:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/ecf686508e7fe0e4454085c9af1c06ce613ebf9dc1ef4c5bc2f0f93a146a8a23.png)\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/456f4dde4ad17f8738a277bb3a6eb139db35bb20aaa85dba794f2f1730bd1495.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/example-5636.json" "file_sha256" "e30910b7778992a15cc5ad18fb9d993f342cf99a2dddf931becdfd15f4f78487"}] "usage" {"e-5636" [] "q-12551" [] "e-5634" [] "q-5617" [] "e-5727" []} "basis" 1509 "retractions" [[17592186096975 :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A) = \\frac{2}{7}$ and $P(A∪B) = \\frac{3}{4}$, find $P(B)$."] [17592186096446 :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$, $P(B)=\\dfrac{1}{4}$, and $P(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{7}{12}$, determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events."] [17592186096446 :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = 0\n$$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= P(A) + P(B) - P(A∩B) \\\\\n\\frac{7}{12} &= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} - P(A∩B) \\\\\nP(A∩B) &= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{7}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 3 - 7}{12} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events."] [17592186096437 :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(A\\cup B) = \\dfrac{4}{9}$, find $P(B)$."] [17592186096437 :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= P(A) + P(B) \\\\\nP(B) &= P(A∪B) - P(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{9} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 3}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186096438 :question/problem "Two cards are drawn from a standard $52$-card deck: the ace of hearts and the queen of hearts. These are set apart, and a third card is drawn from the remaining deck. What is the probability that the third drawn card is an ace or a heart?"] [17592186096438 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that the third card is an ace, and let $H$ be the event that the third card is a heart. Then the probability that the third card is an ace or a heart can be represented as $P(A\\cup H)$.\n\nGiven that the ace of hearts is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $H$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e.,\n\n$$\nP(A \\cap H) = 0\n$$\n\nWe can find $P(A)$ and $P(H)$ by computing the necessary ratios. When the third card is drawn, there are $50$ cards in the deck. Among the remaining $50$ cards, there are $13-2=11$ hearts and $4-1=3$ aces. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= \\frac{3}{50} \\\\\nP(H) &= \\frac{11}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪H) &= P(A) + P(H) - P(A∩H) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{50} + \\frac{11}{50} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186094257 17592186094258 17592186094259 17592186094260 17592186094261 17592186095804 17592186095805 17592186095806 17592186095807 17592186095808] "immutable_fields" [[17592186094256 :answer-field/correct] [17592186094256 :answer-field/id] [17592186094256 :answer-field/key] [17592186094256 :answer-field/type] [17592186095803 :answer-field/correct] [17592186095803 :answer-field/id] [17592186095803 :answer-field/key] [17592186095803 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
